The input array is `[10, 20, 30, 40, 50]`. The expected output is `4`.

To solve this problem, we can use a similar dynamic programming approach. We will maintain an array `maxPrices` where `maxPrices[i]` represents the maximum span of prices from the beginning to index `i-1`.

Here is the Java code that implements this solution:

```java
public int calculateSpans(int[] prices) {
    int n = prices.length;
    int[][] dp = new int[n][n];
    
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        dp[i][i] = 1;
    }
    
    for (int len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
        for (int left = 0; left < n - len + 1; left++) {
            int right = left + len - 1;
            if (prices[left] >= prices[right]) {
                dp[left][right] = Math.max(dp[left][left], dp[left+1][right]);
            }
        }
    }
    
    for (int i = n-2; i >= 0; i--) {
        for (int j = i+1; j < n; j++) {
            if (prices[i] <= prices[j]) {
                continue;
            }
            maxSpan = Math.max(maxSpan, dp[i][j]);
        }
    }
    return maxSpan;
}
```

